Technical Constraints & Rules
Please strictly respect the following instructions during the conversion:

1. Core Conversion & Timeframes:
Convert all indicator-specific code to strategy-specific code. Do not use any syntax or functions that a TradingView Strategy does not support. Preserve any existing timeframe (MTF) logic and explicitly handle gaps in the code so the semantic behavior remains identical to the original indicator.

2. Visuals & Plotting:
If the original indicator plots data, the strategy must plot the exact same data to preserve the visuals. Explicitly set all plot offsets to 0.

3. Strategy Declaration & Naming:
Start the script with the exact strategy declaration line below. Replace "NAME" with the original indicator's name, but you must prefix it with "AI - ". Do not set or alter any other strategy settings outside of this line; leave them at their defaults.
strategy("AI - NAME", overlay=true, calc_on_every_tick=false, initial_capital=1000, default_qty_type=strategy.percent_of_equity, default_qty_value=100, commission_type=strategy.commission.percent, commission_value=0.1, slippage=3, pyramiding=1, margin_long=0, margin_short=0, fill_orders_on_standard_ohlc=true)

4. Strict Syntax Rules:

Variables like strategy.commission.percent and strategy.slippage do not exist as standalone calls in Pine Script. Avoid this common mistake. They are only to be set as arguments within the strategy() function (as done in the line above).

Never use line breaks inside function calls, if statements, loops, or when defining variables, as this causes syntax errors. Line breaks should only be used when defining new structural blocks or functions.

5. No Repainting:
Never use lookahead_on (e.g., barmerge.lookahead_on). Cheating or peeking into future data is strictly forbidden.

6. Date Filter Engine:
Build Start Date and End Date inputs into the strategy so the user can filter when trades execute. Set the default date range to start on January 1st, 2018, and end on December 31st, 2069.

